1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,360
This is MATH 239, video 7a. The topic is formal power series: their algebra, inverses, and composition. We use the supplemental notes on formal power series, pages 1 to 4.

2
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:49,169
After this video, you can read a series as a list of coefficients. You can add and multiply series. You can solve the equation A of x times B of x equals C of x, one coefficient at a time. You can decide if an inverse exists, and compute it. You can decide if a composition of two series is a formal power series. And you can prove the fact of assignment problem A2-1. Video 7b covers tutorial and midterm problems, square roots, and Taylor series.

3
00:00:49,619 --> 00:01:15,518
A generating series lists counts. The coefficient of x to the n counts the objects of weight n. We never put a number in place of x. We only add series, multiply series, and read coefficients. A formal power series is a sequence a sub 0, a sub 1, a sub 2, and so on, written with powers of x. The power x to the n only marks the position of a sub n.

4
00:01:15,968 --> 00:01:34,293
The geometric series, 1 plus x plus x squared and so on, is the sequence 1, 1, 1, and so on. In calculus, x equal to 2 gives a sum with no value. As a formal power series, there is no problem. It is only a list of numbers.

5
00:01:34,743 --> 00:01:48,013
A polynomial is also a formal power series. 1 plus 2 x plus x squared is the sequence 1, 2, 1, then zeros. Its coefficients are 0 after some position.

6
00:01:48,463 --> 00:02:03,033
The coefficients can be any complex numbers. When we solve equations with series, fractions and negative numbers appear. This row is a series whose square is 1 plus x. We meet it in video 7b.

7
00:02:03,483 --> 00:02:17,179
Two formal power series are equal if and only if all their coefficients are equal. So one equation between two series is a list of equations between numbers, one for each power of x.

8
00:02:17,629 --> 00:02:35,528
For example, expand 1 plus x times 1 minus x as you expand polynomials. You get 1, plus 0 x, minus x squared. Compare it with 1 minus x squared, column by column. Every column agrees, so the two series are equal.

9
00:02:35,978 --> 00:02:48,202
The notation, the coefficient of x to the k in A of x, means the number a sub k. It is one number, not a series. Here the coefficient of x squared is minus 1.

10
00:02:48,652 --> 00:03:06,871
The constant term has a second name: A of 0. A of 0 is the coefficient of x to the 0, which is a sub 0. It is a name for the constant term. It does not mean that we put 0 into a function. The theorems in this video use this name.

11
00:03:07,321 --> 00:03:20,953
The sum adds the coefficients in each column. Here A of x is the geometric series and B of x is 1 minus x. The sum is 2, then 0, then 1, 1, 1, and so on.

12
00:03:21,403 --> 00:03:43,973
The product multiplies every term of A by every term of B, as for polynomials. The grid shows all the products. Row i is the term a sub i x to the i. Column j is the term b sub j x to the j. The cell i, j holds a sub i b sub j, and it belongs to x to the i plus j.

13
00:03:44,423 --> 00:03:59,122
To find the coefficient of x cubed, collect the cells with i plus j equal to 3. They lie on one purple diagonal: a 0 b 3, a 1 b 2, a 2 b 1, and a 3 b 0.

14
00:03:59,572 --> 00:04:23,167
In general, the coefficient of x to the k in A times B is the sum over i from 0 to k of a sub i times b sub k minus i. This is the product formula. It has exactly k plus 1 terms, so each coefficient is a finite number. The product of two formal power series is always a formal power series.

15
00:04:23,617 --> 00:04:33,558
Here is an example. Find the square of the geometric series. Every coefficient of both factors is 1, so every cell of the grid is 1.

16
00:04:34,008 --> 00:04:45,336
The coefficient of x cubed is the sum on the diagonal i plus j equal to 3. It has four cells, each equal to 1. So the coefficient is 4.

17
00:04:45,786 --> 00:04:59,439
In general, the diagonal for x to the k has k plus 1 cells. So the coefficient of x to the k is k plus 1. The square is 1 plus 2 x plus 3 x squared, and so on.

18
00:04:59,889 --> 00:05:23,335
Now we solve an equation. Example 2.1 asks for a series B of x with 1 minus 2 x, times B of x, equal to 1 plus x. Pause the video and try it. Write B of x with unknown coefficients b sub 0, b sub 1, and so on. Write the right side as 1 plus x plus 0 x squared, and so on.

19
00:05:23,785 --> 00:05:39,038
Expand the left side with the product formula. The known factor has only two nonzero coefficients: 1 and minus 2. So the coefficient of x to the k is b sub k minus 2 b sub k minus 1.

20
00:05:39,488 --> 00:05:53,163
Now compare the columns. For k equal to 0, b sub 0 equals 1. For k equal to 1, b sub 1 minus 2 b sub 0 equals 1, so b sub 1 equals 3.

21
00:05:53,613 --> 00:06:05,261
For k at least 2, the right side is 0. So b sub k equals 2 times b sub k minus 1. This gives 6, 12, 24, 48, and so on.

22
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:24,697
So B of x equals 1 plus 3 x plus 6 x squared, and so on. The coefficient of x to the k is 3 times 2 to the k minus 1. Each equation forced exactly one value. So we found a solution, and we also proved that it is the only solution.

23
00:06:25,147 --> 00:06:34,641
Example 2.2 changes the known factor to x minus 2 x squared. Pause the video and try to solve it with the same method.

24
00:06:35,091 --> 00:06:49,896
The known factor has constant term 0. By the product formula with k equal to 0, the constant term of the product is 0 times b sub 0. That is 0, for every choice of b sub 0.

25
00:06:50,346 --> 00:07:05,855
But the constant term of 1 plus x is 1. Zero is not equal to 1. So no series B of x solves the equation. The notes say: a zero constant term in the known factor is the only problem that can occur.

26
00:07:06,305 --> 00:07:28,343
Theorem 2.1 states the general result. Let A of x and C of x be formal power series. If A of 0 is not 0, then exactly one series B of x satisfies A of x times B of x equals C of x. The notes say that the method of Example 2.1 proves it. Here is that proof.

27
00:07:28,793 --> 00:07:42,147
By the definition of equality and the product formula, the equation means: for every k, the sum over i from 0 to k of a sub i b sub k minus i equals c sub k.

28
00:07:42,597 --> 00:08:06,896
Take the term with i equal to 0 out of the sum. It is a sub 0 times b sub k. Since a sub 0 is not 0, we can divide by it. So b sub k equals c sub k minus the rest of the sum, all over a sub 0. For k equal to 0 the rest is empty, so b sub 0 equals c sub 0 over a sub 0.

29
00:08:07,346 --> 00:08:26,205
The rest of the sum uses only b sub 0 up to b sub k minus 1. For example, b sub 3 uses b sub 2 with a sub 1, b sub 1 with a sub 2, and b sub 0 with a sub 3. Here we need a sub 0 not equal to 0, to divide.

30
00:08:26,655 --> 00:08:38,473
So by induction on k, each b sub k has exactly one value. These values satisfy every equation. So the series B of x exists, and it is unique.

31
00:08:38,923 --> 00:08:55,094
In calculus you saw the formula: 1 over 1 minus x equals 1 plus x plus x squared, and so on. For formal power series, the left side has no meaning yet. We only have a sum and a product. There is no division.

32
00:08:55,544 --> 00:09:15,299
So the notes define an inverse. The inverse of A of x is a series B of x with A of x times B of x equal to 1. Here 1 is the series 1 plus 0 x plus 0 x squared, and so on. We write B of x as 1 over A of x, or A of x to the minus 1.

33
00:09:15,749 --> 00:09:30,170
Check the geometric series G of x. 1 minus x, times G of x, is G of x minus x times G of x. Multiplication by x moves every coefficient one place to the right.

34
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:49,137
Subtract. The constant term is 1. Every other column is 1 minus 1, which is 0. So the product is 1. The geometric series is the inverse of 1 minus x, and the formula from calculus is now a true statement about formal power series.

35
00:09:49,587 --> 00:10:03,753
Theorem 3.1 says which series have an inverse. The inverse of A of x exists if and only if A of 0 is not 0. And if it exists, it is unique. The proof has two cases.

36
00:10:04,203 --> 00:10:23,872
Case 1: A of 0 is not 0. Use Theorem 2.1 with C of x equal to 1. It gives exactly one series B of x with A of x times B of x equal to 1. So the inverse exists and it is unique. Its constant term is 1 over a sub 0.

37
00:10:24,322 --> 00:10:40,962
Case 2: A of 0 is 0. Then for every series B of x, the constant term of the product is 0 times b sub 0, which is 0. But the constant term of 1 is 1. So no B of x works, and A of x has no inverse.

38
00:10:41,412 --> 00:10:55,556
Because the inverse is unique, the notation 1 over A of x names exactly one series. So if two methods both give an inverse, they give the same coefficients. We use this later in this video.

39
00:10:56,006 --> 00:11:14,587
Now compute an inverse. Find the first coefficients of 1 over 1 minus x plus 2 x squared. Pause the video and try it. The constant term is 1, so the inverse exists. The grid shows the products of the terms of A of x with the unknown b sub j.

40
00:11:15,037 --> 00:11:25,427
The right side is 1. So the diagonal for x to the 0 must add to 1. It has one cell, b sub 0. So b sub 0 equals 1.

41
00:11:25,877 --> 00:11:34,815
The diagonal for x to the 1 must add to 0. It is b sub 1 minus b sub 0. So b sub 1 equals 1.

42
00:11:35,265 --> 00:11:47,511
The diagonal for x squared is b sub 2 minus b sub 1 plus 2 b sub 0. It must be 0. So b sub 2 equals 1 minus 2, which is minus 1.

43
00:11:47,961 --> 00:12:00,313
The diagonal for x cubed is b sub 3 minus b sub 2 plus 2 b sub 1, plus 0. So b sub 3 equals minus 1 minus 2, which is minus 3.

44
00:12:00,763 --> 00:12:21,328
So the inverse is 1 plus x minus x squared minus 3 x cubed, and so on. The general rule: b sub n equals minus the sum of a sub i b sub n minus i for i from 1 to n, divided by a sub 0. Later in this video we get the same numbers in a second way.

45
00:12:21,778 --> 00:12:30,354
Here is practice problem P2-1 from Assignment 2. Which of these four series has an inverse? Pause the video and try it.

46
00:12:30,804 --> 00:12:55,551
By Theorem 3.1, look only at the constant term. A of x has constant term 0: no inverse. B of x has constant term 1: the inverse exists. C of x has constant term 3 to the 0, which is 1: the inverse exists. D of x is C of x minus 1, so its constant term is 0: no inverse. These are the posted answers.

47
00:12:56,001 --> 00:13:10,166
We can also find the inverses. The inverse of 1 plus x is 1 minus x plus x squared minus x cubed, and so on. Multiply back to check: each column cancels except the constant term.

48
00:13:10,616 --> 00:13:30,456
The inverse of C of x is the polynomial 1 minus 3 x squared. Check: for m at least 1, the coefficient of x to the 2 m in the product is 3 to the m minus 3 times 3 to the m minus 1, which is 0. The odd columns are 0 in both factors.

49
00:13:30,906 --> 00:13:53,690
The composition A of B of x replaces each x in A of x with the series B of x. So A of B of x is a sub 0, plus a sub 1 times B of x, plus a sub 2 times B of x squared, and so on. This is an infinite sum of series. It is a formal power series only if each coefficient is a finite number.

50
00:13:54,140 --> 00:14:11,484
Here is an example from the notes. Take A of x as the geometric series, and B of x as 1 plus x. Then A of B of x is 1, plus 1 plus x, plus 1 plus x squared, and so on. The table shows the powers of 1 plus x.

51
00:14:11,934 --> 00:14:29,470
Look at the constant terms. Every power of 1 plus x has constant term 1. So the constant term of the composition is 1 plus 1 plus 1, and so on, with no end. This is not a number. So this composition is not a formal power series.

52
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:39,947
The cause is the constant term of B of x. It is 1, not 0. The next theorem shows that a zero constant term fixes the problem.

53
00:14:40,397 --> 00:14:57,847
Theorem 3.2. Let A of x and B of x be formal power series. If B of 0 is 0, then A of B of x is a well defined formal power series. The table shows the powers of B of x equal to x plus x squared.

54
00:14:58,297 --> 00:15:14,511
Here is the proof. B of 0 is 0, so every term of B of x contains x. So B of x equals x times C of x, for a formal power series C of x. For x plus x squared, C of x is 1 plus x.

55
00:15:14,961 --> 00:15:31,345
Put this into the definition. The term a sub k B of x to the k becomes a sub k, x to the k, C of x to the k. So the k-th power starts at x to the k or later. The green staircase marks these zeros.

56
00:15:31,795 --> 00:15:49,757
Now take the coefficient of x to the n. Every term with k greater than n starts after x to the n, so it adds 0. Only the terms with k from 0 to n are left. In the table, column x cubed uses only rows 0 to 3.

57
00:15:50,207 --> 00:16:04,415
So each coefficient is a finite sum of numbers, and A of B of x is a formal power series. The first line of the proof in the notes has a misprint: a sub 2 B of x should be a sub 1 B of x.

58
00:16:04,865 --> 00:16:25,260
Here is an example. A of x is the geometric series, and B of x is x plus x squared. Find the first coefficients of A of B of x. Pause the video and try it. B of 0 is 0, so the composition is well defined. All a sub k are 1, so we add the rows of the table.

59
00:16:25,710 --> 00:16:37,913
For x cubed, add column 3. Rows 0 and 1 give 0. Row 2, x plus x squared, squared, gives 2. Row 3 gives 1. So the coefficient is 3.

60
00:16:38,363 --> 00:16:50,971
Do the same for every column. The column sums are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8. So A of B of x is 1 plus x plus 2 x squared plus 3 x cubed, and so on.

61
00:16:51,421 --> 00:17:01,149
The method: for the coefficient of x to the n, keep the rows k up to n. Multiply the entry in column n by a sub k, and add.

62
00:17:01,599 --> 00:17:11,092
The notes give a second way to find an inverse. Write 1 minus x plus 2 x squared as 1 minus the series x minus 2 x squared.

63
00:17:11,542 --> 00:17:32,897
Then 1 over it is the geometric series with x replaced by x minus 2 x squared. This is a composition with B of x equal to x minus 2 x squared. B of 0 is 0, so by Theorem 3.2 it is well defined. The table shows the powers of B of x up to x cubed.

64
00:17:33,347 --> 00:17:46,531
Add the columns. The constant term is 1. The coefficient of x is 1. For x squared, minus 2 plus 1 is minus 1. For x cubed, minus 4 plus 1 is minus 3.

65
00:17:46,981 --> 00:17:57,349
These are the same coefficients as before, when we used Theorem 2.1. They must agree, because the inverse is unique, by Theorem 3.1.

66
00:17:57,799 --> 00:18:09,980
Assignment problem A2-1. Prove: if A of x times B of x is the zero series, then A of x is 0 or B of x is 0. Pause the video and try it.

67
00:18:10,430 --> 00:18:32,638
Use a proof by contradiction. Suppose both series are nonzero. Let i be the smallest index with a sub i not 0, and let j be the smallest index with b sub j not 0. In the grid, i is 2 and j is 1. All the cells in the rows above i, and in the columns left of j, are 0.

68
00:18:33,088 --> 00:18:48,256
Look at the coefficient of x to the i plus j. By the product formula, it is the sum on the purple diagonal: a sub k times b sub i plus j minus k, for k from 0 to i plus j.

69
00:18:48,706 --> 00:19:10,551
For k less than i, a sub k is 0. For k greater than i, the index i plus j minus k is less than j, so that b is 0. Only the cell with k equal to i is left. So the coefficient is a sub i times b sub j. Both factors are not 0, so the product is not 0.

70
00:19:11,001 --> 00:19:29,881
This contradicts the fact that A of x times B of x is 0. So A of x is 0, or B of x is 0. In words: the lowest terms of two nonzero series multiply to the lowest term of the product. Video 7b uses this fact for square roots.

71
00:19:30,331 --> 00:19:44,817
To recap. A formal power series is a list of coefficients. Two series are equal when every coefficient is equal. The coefficient of x to the k gives one entry, and A of 0 is the constant term.

72
00:19:45,267 --> 00:20:00,691
The product formula adds the cells on one diagonal of the product grid. With it, Theorem 2.1 solves A of x times B of x equals C of x, one column at a time, when A of 0 is not 0.

73
00:20:01,141 --> 00:20:30,239
Theorem 3.1: an inverse exists if and only if the constant term is not 0, and it is unique. Theorem 3.2: a composition A of B of x is a formal power series when B of 0 is 0. The geometric series then gives inverses. And by A2-1, a product of two nonzero series is not zero. Video 7b solves tutorial and midterm problems, and covers square roots and Taylor series.
